        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12508555/comment/13044431",
          "id": "13044431",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=stillalex",
            "name": "stillalex",
            "key": "alex.parvulescu",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133"
            },
            "displayName": "Alex Deparvu",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/Los_Angeles"
          },
          "body": "I have finally figured it out. \nIt was my change that triggered this issue, in fact the TokenStream that I was passing to the node's copy was not meant to be consumed more than once, so sometimes the copy consumed the data, leaving nothing else for the real node.\nTo be more exact, when the extraction was slow, it passed to the background. If this happened, the real node would loose its original data, because of the TokenStream.\n\nThe fix was to add 'reset' and 'close' methods to the SingletonTokenStream, so that it can be consumed mode than once.",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=stillalex",
            "name": "stillalex",
            "key": "alex.parvulescu",
